{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789917501,"nonce":"rSgx+HSUjureZcCvczF1oi3Qxws=","title":"sema types an operand as Num from the operation that starts where it does","message":"Reproducer:\n\n    class V {\n      construct new(x) { _x = x }\n      +(o) { V.new(_x + o) }\n      x { _x }\n    }\n    var a = V.new(1)\n    var b = a + 1\n    System.print(b.x)\n\nEvery mode fails with \"unbox: not a number\" at `V.new(1)` (interpreter,\ntiered, cranelift-only). Expected: 2.\n\nTwo things combine:\n\n1. `TypeEnv.exprs` is keyed by span start. A binary operation and its\n   left operand start at the same offset, and the operation's type is\n   recorded last, so `get_expr_type(left.start)` answers with the\n   operation's type. For `a + 1` that is the type of the sum, not of\n   `a`.\n2. `infer_binary_op` returned Num for arithmetic whenever neither\n   operand was known to be a non-Num (\"Num under uncertainty\", meant\n   for hover). So `Any + Num` is Num, and by (1) `a` is Num.\n\nThe MIR builder trusts `is_num()` on both operands to emit an unchecked\n`Unbox` + `AddF64`. The interpreter's Unbox raises; the JIT's is a\nbitcast. Any Wren class with an operator method and a Num literal on\nthe other side hits it: `v * 2`, `p + 1`.\n\nIt is also why module-level loops \"unboxed\" their variables without a\nguard: every `i = i + 1` on a module variable took this path, so the\nJIT never needed a speculation for them; with the typing made sound\nthey need one (the loop-carried promotion with a Num guard at the\nloop's entry).\n\nFix: record expression types by exact span as well\n(`get_expr_type_at`) and have the builder ask for exactly the operand;\nmake arithmetic Num only when both operands are proven Num. Hover\nshows Any for `x.getter * 22` now, which is the honest answer: the\nreceiver's class may define `*`.","files":null}]}